WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
A Canadian observer tracked the moon's swallowing Jupiter by hand with an 8-inch dub-sonion. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 245. SpaceX is lining up a starship catch, Swift has re-entered over Indonesia, a new low-frequency radio-sky map is in, and a cosmic deep dive follows later. Caitlin Beekroft in London, Ontario, as astronomy magazine described the morning, recorded a thin crescent only about 20% illuminated sliding in front of Jupiter on October 6th, 2020.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:38.020
26. Video came from a ZW-O-A-S-I-224-MC planetary camera. She stacked the sharpest frames

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:42.820
to build the finished picture. Observers across much of Eastern North America could see

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:49.291
the same alignment that morning. SpaceX has set Flight 15 for Friday, November 13th.

00:00:49.291 --> 00:00:54.891
Tesla Roddy reported, aiming for the first recovery of the ship's stage. The attempt opens a new

00:00:54.891 --> 00:00:59.951
phase of Starship work built around reusing both the booster and the ship. The Neil

00:00:59.951 --> 00:01:04.131
Royal Garell's Swift Observatory from the National Aeronautics and Space Administration,

00:01:04.411 --> 00:01:10.551
or NASA, came back into Earth's atmosphere on November 4th at 739 Eastern, over the

00:01:10.551 --> 00:01:12.251
North Sumatra region of Indonesia.

00:01:12.891 --> 00:01:15.651
The satellite had operated for nearly 22 years.

00:01:16.251 --> 00:01:21.131
Its ultraviolet optical telescope gathered the first 1 million images that later fed a

00:01:21.131 --> 00:01:22.331
2018 mosaic.

00:01:23.051 --> 00:01:28.271
Swift flew an X-ray telescope with a gamma-ray telescope to watch the fastest, most energetic

00:01:28.271 --> 00:01:33.191
explosions in the universe. The latest solar maximum sped up the decay of its orbit,

00:01:33.711 --> 00:01:38.691
and a rescue attempt to raise that orbit failed. On the way down, it burned like a shooting star,

00:01:39.151 --> 00:01:44.540
and the archive it left will keep feeding research for years. An international team led by

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:50.520
Lyden University, according to Fizz.org, charted a region 25 times the area of the full moon.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:57.360
The picture picked up almost 63,000 radio sources, from active galactic nuclei to galaxies busy

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.640
forming stars. The results were accepted for publication in astronomy and

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:06.920
astrophysics and are available on archive. The low-frequency array or low-far

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:11.876
delivered the most sensitive picture yet made at those low frequencies. The

00:02:11.876 --> 00:02:16.016
California Science Center opens the Samuel Oshin Air and Space Center on November

00:02:16.016 --> 00:02:21.796
13th. The Daily Trojan reported adding 200,000 square feet at Exposition Park.

00:02:22.376 --> 00:02:26.516
The project was built over four decades in partnership with NASA's Jet Propulsion

00:02:26.516 --> 00:02:31.776
laboratory. Almost doubles the room given to exhibits and brings 100 hands-on exhibits

00:02:31.776 --> 00:02:37.956
plus 100 artifacts. Three galleries take on how aviation works, the hardware of spaceflight,

00:02:38.496 --> 00:02:42.376
and space shuttle Endeavour standing upright with its external tank and boosters.

00:02:42.996 --> 00:02:48.096
Kenneth Phillips, curator there for nearly 40 years, said the point is to show how flying and

00:02:48.096 --> 00:02:53.676
exploring space have evolved, and how we have evolved with them. Guests can program a miniature

00:02:53.676 --> 00:02:57.436
Mars rover and walk through a replica of the International Space Station.

00:02:57.996 --> 00:03:03.276
David Barnhart of USC noted that standing the orbiter on end makes people look up at

00:03:03.276 --> 00:03:07.236
the scale of the hardware, and he stressed that touching the exhibits gives students

00:03:07.236 --> 00:03:10.136
and adults a gut-level link to aerospace engineering.

00:03:10.536 --> 00:03:16.956
The Korean Air Aviation Gallery includes a section of a Boeing 747 with its internal structure

00:03:16.956 --> 00:03:17.636
left open.

00:03:18.176 --> 00:03:23.336
The Kent Cresa Space Gallery holds rockets, robotic spacecraft, and a walkthrough section

00:03:23.336 --> 00:03:28.976
of a solid rocket booster. The Samuel Oshin Shuttle Gallery sets endeavor upright beside its

00:03:28.976 --> 00:03:34.096
tank and boosters so the full launch stack is visible. The center will offer paid high school

00:03:34.096 --> 00:03:39.256
internships and connect students to aerospace opportunities across Los Angeles, and sitting in

00:03:39.256 --> 00:03:45.031
South Central Los Angeles it sits within reach of several nearby communities and schools. A new

00:03:45.031 --> 00:03:50.551
processing method described on the space subreddit rings extra detail out of existing James Webb Space

00:03:50.551 --> 00:03:55.591
telescope observations. It is aimed at taking direct images of planets around stars.

00:03:56.111 --> 00:04:00.891
Standard pipelines miss that detail and archive datasets, and this approach pulls it back.

00:04:01.451 --> 00:04:05.811
A Hyderabad startup has opened India's first privately-run orbital debris site,

00:04:06.311 --> 00:04:10.931
India News Network reported, with work focused on tracking and managing that material.

00:04:11.351 --> 00:04:15.191
The center will help watch and cut down the growing clutter in low-earth orbit.

00:04:15.191 --> 00:04:22.372
The Hubble Space Telescope captured Messier-30 in Capricornis, a globular cluster Fizz.org placed

00:04:22.372 --> 00:04:27.592
in the Milky Way Halo, as a NASA and European Space Agency or ESA project.

00:04:28.272 --> 00:04:32.712
Harlow Shapley once used variable stars and globular clusters like this to demonstrate

00:04:32.712 --> 00:04:35.312
that the Sun does not sit at the center of the galaxy.

00:04:35.932 --> 00:04:40.772
Hubble used the advanced camera for surveys and wide field camera 3 through several observing

00:04:40.772 --> 00:04:41.332
programs.

00:04:41.332 --> 00:04:45.772
The stacked picture covers ultraviolet, visible, and near-infrared light.

00:04:46.212 --> 00:04:51.932
Earlier campaigns had already surveyed dozens of Milky Way globular clusters in red and near-infrared,

00:04:52.252 --> 00:04:56.732
and a later run came back to Messier 30 with three filters tuned to bluer light.

00:04:57.589 --> 00:05:03.509
Pack a mid-sized city's worth of stars into a space smaller than the hop from Earth to the nearest star beyond the sun,

00:05:04.029 --> 00:05:06.949
and you have the kind of crowding a globular cluster can manage.

00:05:06.949 --> 00:05:12.569
Messier 30 jams, tens of thousands to millions of stars into a sphere only tens of light

00:05:12.569 --> 00:05:17.509
years across at its outer edges, and a planet tucked inside would see a night sky burning

00:05:17.509 --> 00:05:19.149
with thousands of suns at once.

00:05:19.909 --> 00:05:25.629
Clusters like this assembled when the universe was young, between 11.5 and 13.5 billion years

00:05:25.629 --> 00:05:25.929
ago.

00:05:26.469 --> 00:05:31.409
Their stars still hold the chemical marks of that time, having lived almost alone ever since.

00:05:31.409 --> 00:05:38.489
ride with a photon leaving one of those ancient suns and after 27,500 light years you would cross

00:05:38.489 --> 00:05:44.209
the Milky Way's halo and arrive here, carrying news of elements forged before our own sun existed.

00:05:44.929 --> 00:05:50.209
Repeated Hubble visits pulled out stars down to 20% of the sun's mass and followed how different

00:05:50.209 --> 00:05:56.149
stellar populations moved inside the cluster. Frames that catch blue straggler stars also chart how

00:05:56.149 --> 00:06:01.189
the cluster's structure has shifted because a long baseline turns tiny changes in position

00:06:01.189 --> 00:06:06.189
into something measurable. The bluer light proved critical for studying exotic objects

00:06:06.189 --> 00:06:09.949
and the multiple stellar populations that can share one globular cluster.

00:06:10.549 --> 00:06:14.449
The newest frames tracked how those populations had moved over the 15 years

00:06:14.449 --> 00:06:18.729
since Hubble first imaged them. Messier 30 and dozens of other clusters

00:06:18.729 --> 00:06:21.789
travel around the Milky Way against the galaxy's own spin.

00:06:22.389 --> 00:06:26.509
That reverse path leaves a direct question, where they born here or pulled

00:06:26.509 --> 00:06:29.449
from smaller galaxies that the Milky Way later absorbed.

00:06:29.449 --> 00:06:34.469
Chemical patterns and orbital directions alone cannot yet tell those two histories apart.

00:06:34.969 --> 00:06:39.649
Separating them would take velocity measurements inside the cluster finer than Hubble can make,

00:06:40.049 --> 00:06:44.769
or fresh models linking those star motions to when galactic mergers actually took place.

00:06:45.469 --> 00:06:49.669
Clusters that formed inside the Milky Way keep the young galaxies' original chemistry,

00:06:50.109 --> 00:06:54.109
while captured ones may carry signatures from entirely different stellar environments.

00:06:54.769 --> 00:06:59.289
We can watch the clusters move and measure what their stars are made of, but nobody knows

00:06:59.289 --> 00:07:04.169
exactly when those captures happened, or how many of them marked the galaxy's early growth.

